For \(y^2=x^3-x\), primes \(103,107\) gave \(N=11021\), traces \(0,0\), \(a(N^2)=11021\), and \(M=11232\).

**PROVED by finite computation — the supplied exceptional input.** For model \([0,-1,1,-10,-20]\) of 11a1,
\[
p=54443,\ q=62819,\ N=3420054817,\ M=3420029925.
\]
Local curve orders are \(54795,62415\). Twist groups are cyclic of orders \(54093,63225\); both exponents divide odd \(M\). This independently verifies membership in \(X_E\).

**PROVED by finite computation — sextic repair on \(X_E\).** For \(y^2=x^3+2\),
\[
p=151,\ q=181,\ N=27331,\quad
M=29925=3^2\cdot5^2\cdot7\cdot19.
\]
Curve orders are \(171,175\); twist invariants are \((133)\) and \((63,3)\). Their exponents \(133,63\) divide odd \(M\), so this is in \(X_E\).

| \(r\) | Killed pairs | Non-killed pairs | Singular \(c=0\) | All pairs |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 151 | 12000 | 10650 | 151 | 22801 |
| 181 | 10980 | 21600 | 181 | 32761 |

PARI group-invariant calculations and independent enumeration of every affine pair reproduced both rows. The terminal mixed probability is exactly
\[
\frac{12000\cdot21600+10650\cdot10980}{27331^2}
=\frac{376137000}{746983561}
=0.5035412017587895\ldots.
\]
With `Random(25)`, trial 2 used
\[
(x,y,c)=(502,7013,22891)
\]
and exposed factor **181** during the group law. Intermediate splits can increase the observed success rate above the terminal mixed probability.

